Define a reaction rate and infer a law from appropriate evidence (@gc-rate-laws: 0, is #chapter, #part-of @general-chemistry, #source "../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94", #source "../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ", #source "../sources/MIT-5-111SC.lingua#r_312Z44VT67CCX60KG27PWM6NZ8", #general-chemistry "Integrated-laws-and-estimation.lingua#r_2WZTK2PWSJ27S4PB9K0BYCAJF3", #water-and-sanitation "../../../utilities/water-and-sanitation/treatment/A-mixed-tank-responds-to-its-entire-inlet-history.lingua#r_44K797MS8S8T2RC8B9ST195TQT") { r_2RAAQPESP3HCEDY95PCFTXVVYZ
**Read:** [Chemistry 2e — Flowers, Theopold, Langley, and Robinson](../sources/Chemistry-OpenStax.lingua#r_02Y5N2VMTTPBDSNYPD4Z26EZ94), chapter 12 in full; [Chem1 General Chemistry Virtual Textbook — Stephen Lower](../sources/Chem1-Lower.lingua#r_0NFQA43WZVMECZY6SKZJMRXWBZ), Chemical kinetics and reaction mechanisms completely; [Principles of Chemical Science — MIT 5.111SC, Fall 2014](../sources/MIT-5-111SC.lingua#r_312Z44VT67CCX60KG27PWM6NZ8), Unit V’s rate-law lectures and problems.

A reaction rate describes a composition change over time under stated conditions and a specified reaction account. For a simple constant-volume single reaction, normalize species concentration derivatives by their stoichiometric coefficients so they describe the same extent rate. Changing volume, flow, or several simultaneous reactions requires a fuller balance.

A rate law is a model relating rate to concentrations, activities, temperature, and other relevant influences. Empirical reaction orders are inferred within a regime; they do not automatically equal coefficients in the overall balanced equation. An elementary-step model supplies a different level of information and needs its own evidence.

Keep units of the rate constant consistent with the chosen law. A first-order constant has inverse-time units, while a second-order concentration law uses a different dimension. Numerical values copied between minutes and seconds, or between amount-concentration units, can silently change predictions.

**Work:** use an original small hypothetical initial-rate table to distinguish two candidate laws, stating which variables were controlled and how uncertainty affects the discrimination. Check a proposed law against units and limiting cases. Explain why changing temperature or catalyst condition at the same time confounds an inferred concentration order.

Read the full kinetics chapter and Chem1 kinetics unit, working the problems. The later integrated-law, mechanism, and transport Records deepen how observations support a chemical explanation. A successful fitted curve is useful evidence but needs more than visual similarity to identify the underlying process.

**Continue:** [Derive and test integrated rate laws instead of selecting a convenient straight line](Integrated-laws-and-estimation.lingua#r_2WZTK2PWSJ27S4PB9K0BYCAJF3).

